<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    DnnMigration.IntegrationTests (AAP 0.4.1.1 / 0.5.1.5)

    Hosts the real DnnMigration.Api request pipeline in process through
    WebApplicationFactory<Program>, which is why Program.cs in DnnMigration.Api is
    declared `public partial` in the global namespace. Every test class in this
    project carries [Trait("Category", "Integration")]; Validation Gate 5 selects
    exactly this suite with a "Category=Integration" test filter, a mechanism AAP
    0.9.4 records as verified by execution.

    Test-database strategy (AAP 0.6.1): Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory and
    Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite are the DEFAULT stores, so the suite runs
    with no container runtime present. Testcontainers.MsSql is available for
    schema-faithful runs where a Docker daemon exists but is deliberately NOT the
    default.
  -->

  <!--
    BUILD POLICY IS INHERITED. DO NOT RE-DECLARE IT AND DO NOT SHADOW IT.

    TargetFramework, LangVersion, Nullable, ImplicitUsings, TreatWarningsAsErrors,
    NoWarn, GenerateDocumentationFile and EnforceCodeStyleInBuild all arrive from
    backend/Directory.Build.props. AAP 0.1.1.3 mandates a single props file so the
    policy applies uniformly across all six projects rather than being duplicated
    per .csproj. MSBuild stops at the FIRST Directory.Build.props it finds walking
    upward, so adding a Directory.Build.props or Directory.Build.targets to this
    folder or to backend/tests/ would silently shadow the parent and build this one
    project under a different policy.

    AssemblyName and RootNamespace are intentionally left at the SDK default,
    $(MSBuildProjectName), which already resolves to DnnMigration.IntegrationTests.
    IsTestProject is set automatically by Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk.props, so it is not
    restated here either. IsPackable is the only property this project adds.
  -->

  <!--
    ANALYZER HAZARD, RECORDED FOR EVERY AUTHOR OF A TEST IN THIS PROJECT.

    xunit.analyzers ships transitively with xunit and reports its xUnit#### rules as
    warnings. The inherited TreatWarningsAsErrors=true, plus the warnaserror switch
    that Validation Gate 1 passes explicitly, promote every one of them to a build
    ERROR. They must be fixed in the test, never suppressed: only CS8618 and CS1591
    are suppressed anywhere in this solution and AAP 0.6.3 is categorical that "no
    other warning is suppressed". No xUnit#### code may be added to a NoWarn
    property, to this file, to backend/.editorconfig or to any new config file.

    Verified against xunit.analyzers 1.18.0 (the version xunit 2.9.3 resolves), these
    four are warning-severity by default and therefore BREAK THE BUILD here:
      xUnit1031 forbids blocking on a Task inside a test, which turns AAP Rule T6
                (async all the way down, no .Result, no .Wait(), no
                GetAwaiter().GetResult()) into a compile-time guarantee. It bites
                hardest in this project because every HttpClient and EF Core call is
                awaitable. Every test method must be `async Task`, never `async void`.
      xUnit1012 rejects a null [InlineData] value bound to a non-nullable
                parameter, interacting directly with Nullable=enable.
      xUnit1026 rejects an unused theory parameter.
      xUnit2013 requires Assert.Single or Assert.Empty over a count assertion.
    Two further rules worth knowing are info-severity by default, so they surface in
    the IDE but are NOT promoted to errors: xUnit1004 (a test skipped on purpose) and
    xUnit1033 (a test class that ignores a collection fixture it declares). Treat
    both as review findings rather than build failures.

    CS8618 being suppressed is convenient here: it covers the uninitialised
    non-nullable fixture fields that the xUnit fixture pattern makes unavoidable.

    Restore diagnostics are promoted the same way, so any NUxxxx must be fixed by
    aligning versions rather than by suppressing it.
  -->

  <!--
    THREE EF CORE PROVIDERS ARE REACHABLE FROM THIS PROJECT.

    SqlServer arrives transitively through DnnMigration.Infrastructure while
    InMemory and Sqlite are referenced below. Referencing all three is legal;
    applying two Use* calls to the SAME DbContextOptions is not, and EF Core throws
    InvalidOperationException ("Only a single database provider can be registered")
    when it happens. A test fixture must therefore REMOVE the existing provider
    registration before adding its own, never layer one on top.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Exact version pins only (AAP 0.6.1 and the 0.8.3 deterministic-restore
    baseline): no floating range, no wildcard, no version-less PackageReference.
    Every Microsoft.* 8.x package is pinned to the 8.0.29 patch line so it matches
    the installed Microsoft.NETCore.App / Microsoft.AspNetCore.App shared
    frameworks recorded in AAP 0.1.2.4. Ordered alphabetically for reviewability.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="FluentAssertions" Version="6.12.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Moq" Version="4.20.72" />
    <PackageReference Include="Testcontainers.MsSql" Version="3.10.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    EXACTLY ONE ProjectReference (AAP Rule T1 and the 0.8.3 Clean Architecture
    baseline). Application, Infrastructure and Domain arrive transitively through
    the API; naming them explicitly would blur the dependency direction that the
    reference graph exists to enforce.

    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing supplies the Microsoft.AspNetCore.App
    framework reference needed to host Program in process, so this project stays on
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk and adds no FrameworkReference of its own. The referenced
    project must keep emitting DnnMigration.Api.dll, the filename that
    docker/api.Dockerfile hard-codes in its ENTRYPOINT.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DnnMigration.Api\DnnMigration.Api.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
